## Idea 1: Clone Mobile Apps with Free Software

Create clone applications of the big ones like _Uber_, _GlassDoor_,
_Pedidos YA_, but focused on the Chilean community and 100% free.

### Example: An Uber Clone

Imagine the following: you have an open source application that does exactly
what Uber does, but for public transportation.
All the taxis in Chile could use it. This way the main problems of the
current way of catching a taxi would be solved. You would know the route,
the price, the person driving, you would get a message when the taxi is
ready, and it would be possible to pay with your credit card.

### Why a free and open source application?

By creating this application with open source code and a free license (I
recommend GPL3), the world would be a better place. Other countries can use
the technology and improve it. Imagine if all the countries that have
problems with Uber allocated some funds to develop an open source Uber clone.
Anyone who wants to improve their local public transportation could easily
implement a solution, not only for taxis but for every kind of transport.
Example: a rural area that only has one bus all day. Now the people who use
that bus could benefit from knowing when the bus will arrive or whether it
had any problem.

### Why should Chile do it?

Chile has many great developers and Santiago is growing exponentially in the
technological area.
The government also has initiatives like <http://kitdigital.gob.cl> and
<https://github.com/e-gob> that try to bring standards to public applications
and websites.

A global non-profit open source organization like Mozilla
<https://www.mozilla.org/es-ES/> should be created, where the focus is on
creating better ways to connect people with public transportation through
the creation of free and open source applications that rival Uber and
friends in quality.

Chile should take the first step to create an Uber clone and other open
source apps. Other countries could finance this application by donating
money to the worldwide non-profit organization.

Better public transportation is possible if all countries join together and
create an open source alternative.

## Idea 2: Create Municipal / Provincial Banks

It is public knowledge that Chile is an ultra-centralized country.
Where most of the television news is about Santiago.
The resources of the regions go straight to the capital, leaving very little
in them to be able to make improvements in the cities and the lives of the
people.

This is a small idea to try to change and improve the current situation.

The Chilean banking system is in the hands of a few.
Normally located in Santiago. The idea of having a municipal bank is that
the people who live in each commune can buy in stores adhered to the system,
save, ask for loans, pay the bills and taxes, pay for transportation, among
other things. Using nothing more than the local banking system.

Imagine that every citizen of Chimbarongo had a municipal Redcompra type
card with which they can buy in all the stores of the city without needing
cash. They have money in their personal municipal account.
Obviously, if they go to another city they could buy with this card if that
city implements its own compatible banking system.
They could also transfer their funds to other municipal or traditional
banking systems.

Having a municipal banking system will allow the different communes to have
additional funds for the improvement of the city, and better control over
the destination of those funds can be created.

Example: I have a municipal savings account where I choose the investments I
want my money to go to, from paving streets, improving squares, schools,
hospitals, etc.
Instead of investments being made in unknown companies abroad, the
investments are local and aimed at improving the lives of the people of the
city I live in.

Also, every business can operate with this banking system without having to
resort to third parties to be able to accept debit cards and online
payments.

### How to achieve it?

Having a system like the one presented in the idea is possible.
But it requires the creation of a series of free license (AGPL3) and free
banking software. Oriented so that each municipality can implement it
following a common pattern. Low cost, without depending on external
institutions.

One possibility is to use a cryptocurrency with its blockchain system to be
able to validate the transactions and have traceability of the operations.
But this cryptocurrency should not be an existing external one, but one
created exclusively for this purpose, since its fiat monetary value should
not fluctuate due to external factors.

The ideal would be to have one or several companies, or a foundation that
advise each municipality on the implementation of this system.
Providing training and offering world-class support.

It is also necessary that each municipality gives importance to technology.
Besides being flexible for the implementation of new ideas.

Implementing a municipal banking system is possible,
but in addition to the technological difficulty it implies, the bureaucratic
difficulty must also be considered.
Currently Chile is overloaded with systems and people that hinder
innovation and continuous improvement.

Let's hope that in the near future ideas like this one can be implemented.

## Idea 3: Create a State University of Informatics and Technology

Similar to the [Belarusian State University of Informatics and Radioelectronics](https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universidad_Estatal_Belarusa_de_Inform%C3%A1tica_y_Radioelectr%C3%B3nica).

The Chilean State must create an entity oriented to train professionals
capable of creating and maintaining technological systems used inside and
outside the country.

The university must be free. Professionals will pay for their education
through the commitment to work for the state during a reasonable period of
time.

This university must be in charge of advising the state in the area of
informatics and new technologies. All the studies carried out must be
released so that any person can use them, since they have been financed
with state money.

The technologies used by the state and taught at the university must be
Open Source and freely accessible. Under no circumstances should they depend
on foreign companies that grant licenses for their use (e.g.: Oracle,
Microsoft, IBM).

The state must eliminate paying royalties to foreign and national companies
for private technologies. That money must be destined to finance support,
creation and maintenance of Open Source and Free technologies. This means
that all state systems must be released in their source code, so that every
Chilean can offer help and improve them if they wish. If a free alternative
does not exist, it is imperative that the university develops a solution
that does not depend on private companies.

The university will have a Datacenter oriented to offer a service similar to
Amazon Web Services. Where state systems do not depend on foreign and
private servers for their operation, increasing the security of Chile's
information and decoupling its dependence on other countries.

The university will standardize the technologies used in state systems,
providing support and training in their use. It will also be in charge of
creating and maintaining state software (sii, registro civil, hospitales,
educativos, políticos, etc).

By having a free state university of informatics and technology, in charge of
both the training of professionals and the creation and maintenance of state
systems, the country will have people capable of facing the challenges of
the 21st century. Chile will also put an end to its dependence on raw
materials as its main source of income.

## Idea 4: Expand the Use of Trams

In Melbourne, Australia there is a means of transport called the Tram. It
is similar to the trolleys of Valparaíso, but they have some notable
differences.

First of all they use wheels adapted to railway tracks. This seems like an
excellent idea to me because they allow showing cars and pedestrians which
are the paths along which the trams travel, allowing them to be more alert
when moving through the streets. Another benefit of these wheels is that
they have no air, they do not puncture, which delivers a lower maintenance
cost and reduces the waste of broken parts.

Another difference with the trolleys of Valpo, is that the trams have
defined stops. They only stop to pick up and drop off passengers at these
stops. Without exception (maybe in emergencies).

![The Trams present in Melbourne since 1915](trams.webp)

They also have a free travel area. If you get on and off within this area,
the trip is totally free of charge. If you get on or off outside this area,
then you must use your Myki card which is similar to the Bip. The free area
covers a large part of the center of Melbourne, facilitating the movement of
people.

The buses and shared taxis still exist, but their number is very small,
since the trams cover a large part of the area and have a reasonable cost.
Besides, there is an App to know more about the trams and the trip.
<http://yarratrams.com.au/tramtracker>

![Trams and Cars on the same road](trams2.webp)

The trams use the same roads as the cars. Unlike a train, which has larger
and exclusive railway tracks, the trams use tracks at pavement level. This
allows any vehicle to transit through the same space as a tram. The trams
also respect the same traffic lights as the cars. Both means of transport
can coexist without problems.

![Tram on a bridge](trams3.webp)

If Chile implemented a system similar to the Trams in all cities, not only
in Santiago, it would be enormously reducing pollution (the trams, just like
the trolleys, run on electricity). It would also greatly repair the big
problem the capital has with a crowded metro, air pollution and rush hour
traffic jams. Ideally they would be manufactured in Chile, to reduce costs
and supply local needs.

## Idea 5: Weekly Pay

Waiting 30 days for a little money stagnates Chile.

This is something that seems like a real necessity to me and it is easy to
implement. Simply stop paying people on a monthly basis and have them start
receiving payment on a weekly basis.

Weekly pay is the custom in countries like the United States, Australia and
others of the first world. Having a weekly source of income, money flows
faster, you can pay the bills, do the shopping and other activities without
waiting so long to have monetary resources.

### How to achieve it?

Well, the legal structure of Chile allows this type of remuneration. The
ideal would be that your contract stipulated the payment and the social
security contributions as is the custom, but that it also defined the
periodicity of the deposits in a weekly format.

You could do the experiment by asking for a weekly advance (for 4 weeks) of
the salary in a month and see if this remuneration format suits you better.

If most jobs in Chile implemented it there would be greater speed in the
flow of money and it would accelerate the growth of the country.

## Idea 6: Online Queues (2020)

The other day I had to go pick up the medicine for my grandfather at the
pharmacy of the public hospital. Unfortunately, with the way things are with
the pandemic afflicting the world, I had to wait about 3 hours to be served
(and that is arriving super early, since it could have been even longer).

Nowadays there are many ways and applications to request turns, but many
public places still work with systems that do not have online information
and you are forced to be present to know when it is your turn and not lose
it. Probably because those online systems require million-dollar investments
and a giant infrastructure change.

As a palliative measure I put together this simple software that takes a
screenshot of the computer screen and displays it on a web page, which
refreshes every 15 seconds.

- [https://github.com/NinjasCL/screensharer](https://github.com/NinjasCL/screensharer)

For it to work they only need to install this software on the computer in
charge of displaying the information on the television of the place. Once
configured, they can share the special url and the same information from
the television screen will be shown on a website.

![Waiting for a turn at a clinic in 2020](turnos.webp)

This way people will be able to access the information from their computer
or smartphone and not be forced to wait in one place and risk getting sick.

It is my grain of sand to help reduce the contagions of this disease.

Hopefully someone from the IT department of the places that serve the public
can know about this solution and implement it. If you know someone from that
area please share this idea with them :).

## Idea 7: Municipal Rental Service

Renting a house or apartment is a complicated process.
You need to have a lot of paperwork and money.
I believe a service like _Netflix_ to which you pay a fixed price would be a
solution.

You may say that _AirBnB_ solves this, but no!.
That service provides access to rooms and couch surfing,
but it is not what I propose.

In Chile renting a house or apartment is truly a hellish experience.
Landlords and real estate agencies put up many barriers to a dignified
rental. Especially in crowded cities like Santiago. They ask you to have at
least 1 year of stable work with a full-time contract, like 3 times the
price of the rent up front, and sometimes they discriminate by nationality
or simply by how you are.

On the websites obsolete, ugly photographs are used, or no photographs at
all, there is no real time information about the state of the rental.
Basically a really annoying experience.

### What do we propose?

A person or a family will pay a fixed price that will allow different house
rental options.
As a plan, you can subscribe, for example, Basic, Premium, Gold.
This plan comes with all the expenses of the house included like water,
electricity, gas, internet (with consumption limits according to the
subscribed plan).
The family only talks with the House Rental Service and the House Rental
Service talks with the owners.

The owners sign a contract with the House Rental Service and not with the
family. If the family wants a house, they simply choose the houses available
within the plan and move in.
Families and owners can rate each other.
This is for long-term rentals, minimum one year.

This service can be municipal. Each municipality can have a series of houses
that it can rent as a service and for a limited stay time.

They are not permanent houses for families or individuals, it is simply a
way for municipalities to generate income and allow people to travel through
Chile, fostering remote work.

### The benefits

A family or individual will only have to worry about paying the service.
It will not be necessary to have a contract for each landlord or another
company like water or electricity. They will simply pay a monthly service
and live. Also, with this method owners can rent their houses and
apartments to families in a simple way and without complications. Since the
contract is with the House Rental Service, the owners will be paid every
month even if the house does not have a family in it (if the House Rental
Service wants exclusivity).
If the family wants to move, they could simply choose a new house and move
forward. You keep paying the same value as the previous home within the
plan. The old house will be available for a new family.

### Characteristics

The House Rental Service should have the following characteristics:

Plan prices similar to Spotify, Netflix or Digital Ocean.
The whole process is done only once with the House Rental Service.

Then the House Rental Service will take care of the owners and save you all
the hassle. Showing the selected list of houses for rent available for
different family needs.

Quality, updated, transparent and detailed information of each home and the
landlord's requirements.

Families will have access to quality homes.
Owners will have access to better rental options.

Moving services like Japan. You and your family want to move?
No problem.

## Idea 8: Anti-Graffiti Protection

Currently there are many buildings that have graffiti.
We are not talking about paintings as such, but those incoherent and not
very aesthetic scratches that some characters put to "mark territory" and
express their discontent with society, the country or something else.

I believe that each municipality, within the operating permit of commercial
premises and the payment of contributions by each neighbor, should require
that houses be free of these scratches.

That the responsibility for cleanliness and upkeep be within each tenant
and neighbor.

If each location has the obligation to maintain the visual cleanliness of
its space, I believe it would be much cleaner.

Maybe when the cleaning truck passes by, they also have the task of
reporting graffiti and dirt in the streets so that the municipality has the
necessary information to oblige people to clean their spaces or otherwise
they would face fines and in the last of cases that the same municipality
cleans, but that the person who owns the space has the obligation to pay for
that service.

A clean city must also be visually clean.
